 ### How is the posted pay read?
 
-From the posting itself, or from the careers site's own pay field where there is one. Only base pay counts: a range that includes bonus, commission or equity is shown as such or left out. When a posting gives a range per level (Microsoft's "IC4" and "IC5", NVIDIA's "Level 4" and "Level 5", the company's own level names), the drawer lists each level's range under **Pay by level**, the card says "· 2 levels", and the headline range runs from the lowest to the highest. When it gives a range for each place (Amazon's pay table, Apple's and Microsoft's ranges for some areas), the drawer lists each place's own range under **Pay by place** and the card says "· N places". A posting that states only a minimum shows it as "from €46K"; an hourly rate that includes the highest shift differential says so ("Incl. top shift pay"), with the stated base beside it when the posting gives one; where a posting states a yearly figure (Austria's minimum, paid in 14 instalments) that figure is used, and the drawer says "Stated as a yearly figure" and quotes the posting. An internship's pay per hour, week or month is shown as posted, also when the posting doesn't say how long the internship lasts; it is never turned into a yearly salary, and a converted figure ("≈ $5,686–6,000 a month") is converted at the same interval. The headline median (the stats line over the chart, and Insights' median and middle half) compares one country's pay, as the chart's rows do: when the roles in view are paid in more than one currency, it is the median of the main one ("base pay median in the US"), and the rest are counted, not pooled. Part-time hourly pay is also shown as a full-time year of 2,080 hours, for comparison only.
